Subject: [#94-5-131] ECON: Interest rates, etc.

X-Message-Reference: #94-5-122
 
Mr John M Casey <jmcasey@HK.Super.NET> writes:
 
>Actually, in theory real rates are a function of people's fears for
>preserving their capital. 
 
  In another theory, the rates are a premium one gets for delayed
consumption - even without any fear, one would prefer to buy whatever
they want now, rather than to wait for another year. You have to pay
people to make them wait and let you use the money.
 
  In still another theory, the real rates reflect the productive ability
of capital - even if everybody is scared to death, they can't expect any
real interest, unless somebody is able to invest their money and actually
make use of it.
 
  In practice, the rate is determined on the market by the amount of
available capital (which depends on the rate itself), productivity of
investments (depends on amounts invested and lots of other things),
and a variety of other economic, legal and psychological factors and
expectations of their future development by every single economic agent.
  
  One could use a large system of differential equations as a generic model
here, but I doubt that any description consisting of a small combination
of words can have more than educational value.
 
  The 'fear' theory may reflect the dawn of macroeconomic ideas but not
much else.
 
  However, the modern theories don't seem to be much more advanced.
 
  I was just reading an article in the last issue of Business Week on
theories of economic growth, which I guess should be considered very
progressive for reviewing 'radical' suggestions of Schumpeter and a small
group of his followers that technological innovations have something
to do with it...   
  Amazing!   Wasn't it evident for the last 30,000 years?
  
  Economists have no idea how to calculate inflation and productivity in
the new areas/products - the heart of modern economic development -... 
so they just ignore all of them, and calculate their factors looking
at conventional industries.   And then they regulate the system they
don't understand: when the real growth rate exceeds their expectations,
the Federal Reserve tries to cool/choke off the 'overheated' economy by
raising interest rates...
  [ I am not saying that the economy can never overheat, just that
having some general idea of what is going on  may be of some use ]
 
 
  Also, speaking of interest rates:
 
since future [supposedly skyroketing] *real* interest rates will be
determined by the growth of the productive value of capital, while
the *nominal* rates will depend on monetary factors, we may see
a 100% real rate turn into %50 deflation with the nominal interest
(taxed by the government) close to 0.  Also, union workers who fought
to have their salaries adjustable to the cost of living, will see
them halve every year...
 
