From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Tue, 10 May 1994 02:38:28 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-132] ECON: Cyphercurrency backing

X-Message-Reference: #94-5-118
 
 Nancy Lebovitz  <nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu> writes:
 
>                                               ... I like the 
> idea of cyphercurrency, but I'm not sure how it can be backed
> with anything. (And if the governments fall, we won't be using
> their currencies.) Is there some way to connect the money to
> a physical object (like a gram of gold) without losing anonymity?
> How well can an economy work if there's a chance that anything
> you use to back the currency might suddenly become a great deal
> more common? (For instance, a gold-backed currency becomes
> relatively worthless if someone discovers a cheap way to refine
> gold from sea water.)
 
 
  I see two major advantages of backing currency by a commodity: 
  
  - stability of the currency thru emission control (the amount of monetary
    mass is/depends_on the amount of the underlying 'currency commodity'.
 
  - stability thru the general agreement to use it, based on perceived
    safety of always being able to exchange the currency back to the
    underlying commodity (or, in case of the direct involvement of the
    commodity in the transactions, return to its primary use).
 
  The problem with it is that the price of the underlying commodity 
immediately increases because it acquires a new artificially assigned value,
and lots of resources start being wasted on production, transportation and
safeguarding of this commodity, while it is being pushed from the original
areas of its application by the prohibitively high costs.
 
  Now, cyphercurrency allows perfect emission control,  which cannot be
achieved with any commodity, with tiny costs of emission, storage and cir-
culation, little danger of theft or loss and no disruption for the economy
(unless somebody is cheating with emission in which case [declaration of]
backing is of no help anyway).
  As for the perceived safety, cyphercurrencies will be backed by existing
currencies, at least for a while, until everybody feels confident; during
that period, 'backing'/connection with other markets will be performed by
some exchange transactions between the currencies; they will be performed
only occasionally are hardly different from buying tangible goods with
cyphercash. The purely cypher-transactions will be completely anonymous...
 
  Also, one should not expect that there will be just one cyphercurrency
issued by one agency and backed by one commodity.
Digitized stocks, bonds, personal promissory notes and other bit strings
of any legal status and backing scheme may circulate freely in the market
and exchange into each other at variable rates.
  
  The only really serious problem here will be taxation :-)

