>From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
>Date:     Mon, 9 May 94 2:00:41 GMT
>Subject: [#94-5-101] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage
>
>...This is a priori, but I would think that low interest rates would
>indicate a low time preference, and allow longer (and hopefully
>more valuable) processes of production.
>
>I'm not saying that long processes are necessarily more valuable,
>but that an economy that includes both them and the shorter
>processes that would be all that exists at the higher interest
>rates should be able to accomplish more.

This is true for the natural rate of interest, as opposed to an
interest rate that is unnaturally low due to intervention by a
central bank.  There is a natural tendency for interest rates to
go down as capital formation occurs, and, thus, for borrowers to
engage in longer production processes to produce higher-order goods.
But if the interest rate is lowered by fiat credit expansion, then
the borrowers engage in the production of goods which there isn't
any real demand for, so when this is realized, the malinvestment
has to be liquidated.  And that, kiddies, is how we get business
cycles.

>From: minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
>Date: Mon, 9 May 94 00:12:45 -0400
>Subject: [#94-5-103] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage
>
>Perhaps this fiddling with a single 'prime rate' is just so constrained
>that it has mischievous consequences.  How about fiddling, instead, with
>the capital gain tax curves.   You can encourage N-year investments by
>holding the gain tax high for the first N years, etc.

They both have perverse effects.  Capital gains taxes discourage the
investment of venture capital into startups - it's no accident that soon
after the Kemp-Roth(?) capital gains tax cuts in 1978 we had a whole lot
of startups like Sun, Apple, etc., and that there have been less since
they were raised again in 1987(?).

Luckily, you don't need to have capital gains taxes or bonds issued by a
central bank in order to have the low interest rates conducive to high,
sustainable, long-term growth.  All you need is free banking.  ("All" he
says!  As if that was easy!)

